<p class="nx-meta"><a href="/notes/">Guides</a><span>History</span><span>7 min read</span></p>
<h1>The horse did not lose to a better horse. It lost to a different job.</h1>
<p class="nx-dek">The horse comparison is the most used analogy in the AI debate. The numbers behind it say more than the slogan does, and not what the slogan claims.</p>
<div class="nx-byline"><span>By <b>Twinthos Research</b></span><span>Published <b><time datetime="2026-10-08">8 October 2026</time></b></span><span>Updated <b><time datetime="2026-10-13">13 October 2026</time></b></span><span>3 sources, all linked</span></div>
</div></header>
<figure class="nx-fig"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="/assets/notes-img/horse-stable-800.webp 800w, /assets/notes-img/horse-stable-1600.webp 1600w" sizes="(max-width:900px) 100vw, 1160px"><img src="/assets/notes-img/horse-stable-1600.jpg" srcset="/assets/notes-img/horse-stable-800.jpg 800w, /assets/notes-img/horse-stable-1600.jpg 1600w" sizes="(max-width:900px) 100vw, 1160px" width="1600" height="900" alt="An empty wooden horse stall in a dim stable, a single shaft of light on the straw and a tractor wheel visible beyond the open door" fetchpriority="high"></picture><figcaption>Illustration generated for this guide. It is not a photograph of a real stable.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="wrap nx-layout">
<aside class="nx-toc" aria-label="Contents"><p>In this guide</p><ol><li><a href="#the-numbers">The numbers</a></li><li><a href="#it-was-slow">It was slow</a></li><li><a href="#the-land-is-the-real-story">The land is the real story</a></li><li><a href="#what-happened-to-the-people">What happened to the people</a></li><li><a href="#what-the-analogy-gets-right-and-wrong">What the analogy gets right and wrong</a></li></ol></aside>
<div class="nx-article">
<section class="nx-take" aria-label="Key takeaways"><h2>The short version</h2><ul><li>Farm horses and mules in the US peaked at <strong>26.7&nbsp;million in 1918</strong> and were about <strong>3&nbsp;million by 1960</strong>, as tractors rose from 920,000 in 1930 to 4.7&nbsp;million.</li><li>The change took about <strong>four decades</strong>. Farmers kept animals for years after buying tractors.</li><li>Feed for the animals needed <strong>93&nbsp;million acres</strong> of cropland at the 1915 peak. By 1960 it needed 5&nbsp;million. That freed land mattered more than the animal count.</li><li>The analogy has limits: horses could not retrain, and people can. Use it for the pace and pattern of change, not as a verdict.</li></ul></section>

<p>"Be the driver, not the horse" is a line that turns up in almost every talk about AI and work. It is a good line. It is also an invitation to look at the data, which tells a slower, odder and more useful story than the slogan does.</p>
<h2 id="the-numbers">The numbers</h2>
<p>The economists Alan Olmstead and Paul Rhode, in a National Bureau of Economic Research paper on the spread of the tractor, give the total number of farm horses and mules as reaching its maximum of <strong>26.7&nbsp;million head in 1918</strong>.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></sup> The US Department of Agriculture's Economic Research Service gives the later path: 18.7&nbsp;million in 1930, 11.6&nbsp;million work animals in 1945 and about 3&nbsp;million in 1960.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-2" aria-label="Source 2">[2]</a></sup></p>
<figure class="nx-chart nxc-vcols" id="c-horse" role="group" aria-labelledby="c-horse-t"><figcaption class="nx-chart-head"><span class="nx-chart-kicker">Chart</span><strong id="c-horse-t">US farm work animals and tractors, millions</strong></figcaption><div class="nx-legend"><span class="nx-key warn"><i></i>Horses, mules and work animals</span><span class="nx-key hi"><i></i>Tractors</span></div><ul class="nx-vcols n4"><li class="nx-vg"><span class="nx-vbars"><span class="nx-vb warn"><b>21.6</b><i style="--h:86.4%"></i></span><span class="nx-vb empty"></span></span><span class="nx-vl">1900</span></li><li class="nx-vg"><span class="nx-vbars"><span class="nx-vb warn"><b>18.7</b><i style="--h:74.8%"></i></span><span class="nx-vb hi"><b>0.9</b><i style="--h:3.7%"></i></span></span><span class="nx-vl">1930</span></li><li class="nx-vg"><span class="nx-vbars"><span class="nx-vb warn"><b>11.6</b><i style="--h:46.4%"></i></span><span class="nx-vb hi"><b>2.4</b><i style="--h:9.6%"></i></span></span><span class="nx-vl">1945</span></li><li class="nx-vg"><span class="nx-vbars"><span class="nx-vb warn"><b>3.0</b><i style="--h:12.0%"></i></span><span class="nx-vb hi"><b>4.7</b><i style="--h:18.8%"></i></span></span><span class="nx-vl">1960</span></li></ul><p class="nx-chart-note">The USDA counts work animals in 1900, horses and mules in 1930 and 1960, and work animals on farms in 1945. Definitions differ slightly by year. Work-animal data stopped after the 1960 Census.</p><p class="nx-chart-src">Source: USDA Economic Research Service, <a href="https://ers.usda.gov/sites/default/files/_laserfiche/publications/44197/13566_eib3_1_.pdf" rel="noopener">EIB-3</a>, from Census of Agriculture data. No tractor figure is given for 1900.</p></figure>
<p>Tractors tell the other half of the story. They grew from about 1,000 on American farms in 1910 to 246,000 in 1920 and 920,000 in 1930, then to 2.4&nbsp;million in 1945 and 4.7&nbsp;million in 1960.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-2" aria-label="Source 2">[2]</a>,<a href="#src-3" aria-label="Source 3">[3]</a></sup></p>
<h2 id="it-was-slow">It was slow</h2>
<p>The headline version is that tractors replaced horses. The record shows a transition that took roughly four decades from peak to near-disappearance. Total animal numbers were already falling in the 1920s, yet there were still 11.6&nbsp;million work animals on farms in 1945.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-2" aria-label="Source 2">[2]</a></sup></p>
<p>Part of the reason is human. Olmstead and Rhode note that many farmers retained their old stock even after adopting machines, and that at least into the 1940s relatively few of the displaced animals were slaughtered: of the 314,000 horses and mules that disappeared from farms in 1940, only 19,000, or 6&nbsp;percent, went to federally inspected meat plants.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></sup></p>
<blockquote class="nx-pull"><p>A technology can be clearly better and still take forty years to finish the job.</p></blockquote>
<h2 id="the-land-is-the-real-story">The land is the real story</h2>
<p>A mature farm horse needed about three acres of cropland a year for its feed. Across 1880 to 1920, farm draft animals consumed the output of roughly 22&nbsp;percent of all cropland harvested, and animals in cities and mines another 5&nbsp;percent.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></sup></p>
<figure class="nx-chart nxc-bars" id="c-acres" role="group" aria-labelledby="c-acres-t"><figcaption class="nx-chart-head"><span class="nx-chart-kicker">Chart</span><strong id="c-acres-t">Cropland used to feed horses and mules, million acres</strong></figcaption><ul class="nx-hbars"><li class="nx-row warn"><span class="nx-lab">1915 peak</span><span class="nx-track"><i style="--w:93.0%"></i></span><span class="nx-val">93</span></li><li class="nx-row "><span class="nx-lab">1930</span><span class="nx-track"><i style="--w:65.0%"></i></span><span class="nx-val">65</span></li><li class="nx-row hi"><span class="nx-lab">1960</span><span class="nx-track"><i style="--w:5.0%"></i></span><span class="nx-val">5</span></li></ul><p class="nx-chart-note">The authors put the released cropland at roughly two-thirds of all cropland harvested in 1920 in the territory of the Louisiana Purchase, and say most of the roughly 80&nbsp;million acres of pasture the animals used was freed too.</p><p class="nx-chart-src">Source: Olmstead and Rhode, <a href="https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w7947/w7947.pdf" rel="noopener">The Diffusion of the Tractor in American Agriculture, 1910 to 1960</a>, NBER Working Paper 7947.</p></figure>
<p>That is the number the slogan misses. The tractor did not only swap one source of power for another. It released tens of millions of acres from feeding the old power source and put them to other uses. The authors say this almost surely depressed crop and livestock prices and added to pressure for government farm programmes.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></sup></p>
<h2 id="what-happened-to-the-people">What happened to the people</h2>
<p>The USDA frames the long run in plain terms. In 1900, farms employed close to half of the US workforce, along with 22&nbsp;million work animals. Today, a small number of large, mechanised farms employ a tiny share of US workers and use about 5&nbsp;million tractors in place of the horses and mules of earlier days. Output grew dramatically, consumers spent a smaller share of income on food, and a large share of the population was freed to enter non-farm occupations.<sup class="c"><a href="#src-2" aria-label="Source 2">[2]</a></sup></p>
<p>That describes the outcome across generations. It does not describe any individual farmhand in 1935. The sources give animal and tractor counts, not a jobs ledger for the transition, so we do not put a number on how many people lost work, or how long it took them to find more.</p>
<h2 id="what-the-analogy-gets-right-and-wrong">What the analogy gets right and wrong</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Right: the pace is uneven.</strong> Adoption is slow where the old way is cheap, familiar or trusted, and fast once the economics tip.</li>
<li><strong>Right: second-order effects dominate.</strong> The freed acreage reshaped farm prices and policy. The first-order swap was only the start.</li>
<li><strong>Wrong: horses could not retrain.</strong> A horse has one job. People move between jobs, and the USDA record shows the workforce moved out of farming, not into unemployment.</li>
<li><strong>Wrong: the horse had no owner.</strong> The decisions were made by farmers weighing costs. Choices about AI are made the same way, by owners and managers, which is where an individual business has leverage.</li></ul>
<aside class="nx-callout reading"><span class="nx-callout-l">Our reading</span><p>The better question than &ldquo;are you the driver or the horse?&rdquo; is which tasks the new tool takes, who was doing them, and whether those people have a route into the new work. The history says the change is real, slower than the slogan, and broader than the first obvious swap.</p></aside>

<section><h2>Common questions</h2><div class="nx-faq"><details><summary>How many horses and mules were there in the US in 1915?</summary><div class="a"><p>Sources differ by year and by what they count. Olmstead and Rhode give the total number of farm horses and mules as peaking at 26.7&nbsp;million in 1918, with the cropland to feed them peaking in 1915 at about 93&nbsp;million acres. The earlier version of this guide gave 26.5&nbsp;million for 1915; that figure has been replaced.</p></div></details><details><summary>When did tractors replace horses?</summary><div class="a"><p>Gradually, over about four decades. US farms had 246,000 tractors in 1920, 920,000 in 1930, 2.4&nbsp;million in 1945 and 4.7&nbsp;million in 1960. Farm work animals fell from 18.7&nbsp;million in 1930 to about 3&nbsp;million in 1960.</p></div></details><details><summary>How much land did horses need?</summary><div class="a"><p>A mature farm horse needed about three acres of cropland a year for feed. In 1915, about 93&nbsp;million acres of cropland went to feeding horses and mules. By 1960 it was 5&nbsp;million.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is the horse a good analogy for AI and jobs?</summary><div class="a"><p>For pace and second-order effects, partly. For the human outcome, no: horses had one use, while people can move between jobs. The history shows a change that was slow and broad, and that moved the workforce out of farming over generations.</p></div></details></div></section>
<section class="nx-src"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li id="src-1"><a href="https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w7947/w7947.pdf" rel="noopener">The Diffusion of the Tractor in American Agriculture: 1910 to 1960 (NBER Working Paper 7947)</a> <span>Alan L. Olmstead and Paul W. Rhode, National Bureau of Economic Research</span><span>Peak animal numbers, feed cropland, retention and slaughter figures.</span></li><li id="src-2"><a href="https://ers.usda.gov/sites/default/files/_laserfiche/publications/44197/13566_eib3_1_.pdf" rel="noopener">The 20th Century Transformation of U.S. Agriculture and Farm Policy (Electronic Information Bulletin 3)</a> <span>USDA Economic Research Service</span><span>Work animal and tractor counts from the Census of Agriculture; workforce context.</span></li><li id="src-3"><a href="http://hsus.cambridge.org/HSUSWeb/essay/pdf/Da.ESS.02.pdf" rel="noopener">Historical Statistics of the United States, Millennial Edition: agriculture essay (series Da623)</a> <span>Cambridge University Press</span><span>Tractor counts for 1910, 1920 and 1930.</span></li></ol></section>
<div class="nx-log"><b>Corrections and updates</b><p>13 October 2026. Rebuilt around two primary sources, the NBER paper and the USDA bulletin. Corrected the peak: the earlier version said 26.5&nbsp;million in 1915; the NBER paper gives 26.7&nbsp;million in 1918 for farm horses and mules, with feed cropland peaking in 1915. Removed an internal note about an earlier draft that had been left in the published text, and added the cropland and pace sections.</p></div>
</div></div>
</article>
<section class="nx-related"><div class="wrap"><h2>Keep reading</h2><div class="nx-cards"><a class="nx-card" href="/notes/wef-jobs-2030/"><span class="imgbox"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="/assets/notes-img/rails-800.webp" sizes="(max-width:700px) 100vw, 380px"><img src="/assets/notes-img/rails-800.jpg" width="800" height="450" alt="A railway track splitting into two lines at points, running across an empty flat landscape under a wide overcast sky" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></picture></span><span class="nx-card-b"><span class="nx-tag">Jobs</span><span class="nx-card-t">170&nbsp;million jobs created, 92&nbsp;million displaced by 2030</span><span class="nx-card-d">The WEF's Future of Jobs Report 2025: a net gain of 78&nbsp;million jobs, and 11 in every 100 workers who need training but are unlikely to get it.</span><span class="nx-card-m">6 min read &middot; 8 October 2026</span></span></a><a class="nx-card" href="/notes/ai-adoption-gap-2026/"><span class="imgbox"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="/assets/notes-img/office-lit-windows-800.webp" sizes="(max-width:700px) 100vw, 380px"><img src="/assets/notes-img/office-lit-windows-800.jpg" width="800" height="450" alt="A glass office building at dusk, a grid of windows almost all dark with a few lit" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></picture></span><span class="nx-card-b"><span class="nx-tag">AI adoption</span><span class="nx-card-t">88&nbsp;percent of organisations use AI. Almost none run agents at scale.</span><span class="nx-card-d">Stanford's 2026 AI Index measures the gap between trying AI and running work through it.</span><span class="nx-card-m">7 min read &middot; 8 October 2026</span></span></a><a class="nx-card" href="/notes/entry-level-jobs-ai-2026/"><span class="imgbox"><picture><source type="image/webp" srcset="/assets/notes-img/ladder-800.webp" sizes="(max-width:700px) 100vw, 380px"><img src="/assets/notes-img/ladder-800.jpg" width="800" height="450" alt="A tall plain wooden ladder leaning against a bare concrete wall, the lowest rungs in window light and the upper rungs in shadow" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></picture></span><span class="nx-card-b"><span class="nx-tag">Jobs</span><span class="nx-card-t">AI is hitting the first rung of the career ladder first</span><span class="nx-card-d">Stanford's latest data: young workers in AI-exposed jobs are 19% below trend, and the cause is fewer hires, not layoffs.</span><span class="nx-card-m">7 min read &middot; 8 October 2026</span></span></a></div></div></section><section class="nx-end"><div class="wrap nx-end-in"><div><h2>Which tasks does the tool take?</h2><p>Twinthos starts there. It runs the repeating admin in a business under clear rules and leaves a person above the line for decisions. The operations audit measures what those tasks are before anything is changed.</p></div><div class="nx-end-act"><a class="nx-btn" href="/operations-audit/">See the operations audit</a><a class="nx-btn ghost" href="/how-it-works/">How it works</a></div></div></section>
